samples.evidence:
In `moveRangeTo`:
```
const expanded = this.expandChangedSrefsWithMergeAliases(changedSrefs);
const dependantsMap = await this.store.buildDependantsMap(expanded);
for (const anchor of unblockedAnchors) {
  expanded.add(anchor);
  if (!dependantsMap.has(anchor)) dependantsMap.set(anchor, new Set());
}
```
The dependants map is built from `expanded` *before* the unblocked anchors are inserted, so the traversal never walks out from the anchor. When the anchor is absent from the map (the normal case — the blocker cell is not referenced by the anchor's formula, cf. the new test where `A4 = MINVERSE(A1:B2)` and the blocker is `A5`) the code installs `dependantsMap.set(anchor, new Set())`, i.e. an explicitly *empty* dependants set. `calculate` → `topologicalSort` therefore re-evaluates the anchor itself but never any cell that references it. A formula such as `=A4*2` (or `=SUM(A4:B5)` over the newly written ghosts) keeps showing the propagated `#REF!` after the drag-move recovers the spill, until some unrelated edit forces a recalculation. The correct seed would be `buildDependantsMap(new Set([...expanded, ...unblockedAnchors]))`. Note the same shape exists pre-existing in `removeData` (line 1194), so this is a consistent-but-wrong contract being extended, not merely copied harmlessly.

```
switch (refusal) {
  case 'merge-source-split': ...
  case 'merge-dest-partial': ...
  default:
    return "Can't autofill across merged cells. Unmerge them first.";
}
```
Because the third member is handled by `default` rather than `case 'merge-autofill'` (+ exhaustiveness `never` check), adding e.g. `'protected-range'` to `RangeOpRefusal` compiles cleanly and shows users the autofill message for an unrelated refusal. `tsc` cannot catch it.

